<entry dateCreated="2020-08-16T16:15:18Z" dateModified="2025-11-12T02:53:19Z" id="aalinagaali_e7e1eb1e-4b79-4ee5-95c8-3f3d8437bba6" guid="e7e1eb1e-4b79-4ee5-95c8-3f3d8437bba6">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>aalinagaali</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>- naṅ: A Burushaski root referring to a mythical or monstrous being, often associated with trouble, burden, or inevitability. 

- aali: In Shina, means “came” (possibly a marker of direction, focus, or convergence). 

- aalinagaali appears to be a compound, metaphorically suggesting a final convergence toward trouble or inevitability—a moment when all options are exhausted and one must face the consequence.</text></form>
</note>
<relation type="Compare" ref="naaṅ2_bb18f2f7-81b5-4bcf-914c-caf509a2b1c2"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>aːlinagaːli</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>آلیࣷ نگالیࣷ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="456f4ffd-001e-4dbe-9e6f-639eef539d93">
<grammatical-info value="Conjunction">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>last resort, eventually, it came down to</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>آخر کار، آخری  اُمید</text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>men ka cheraġa aqaresen, aalinagaali goġa diya. </text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>No one agreed to go; it all came down to you. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
