<entry dateCreated="2019-04-24T14:56:22Z" dateModified="2025-11-14T03:33:26Z" id="ajo uyeśum_c87e9f19-4442-4757-b7e7-33e890085d9b" guid="c87e9f19-4442-4757-b7e7-33e890085d9b">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ajo uyeśum</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="phrase"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>Literally, the phrase means “to see others badly” or “to perceive others negatively.” Though this may not align with standard English idiom, in Burushaski it expresses a culturally grounded sense of emotional rejection or social unwelcomeness.

ajo → bad or badly
yec → see
yeśum __ seeing</text></form>
</note>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>aʒo uyɛʃum</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>اجو اُیࣷیࣷشم</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="6e4739a7-3687-48c8-93b0-b46396e2011e">
<grammatical-info value="Verb/Verbal Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>being inhospitable, disliking people's presence, being resentful, unwelcoming </text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>کسی کو  دل سے قبول نہ کرنا۔ </text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>mi ne haalla chera ġa ne mi ajo miyeci. </text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>When we went to his house he saw us badly. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2019-05-26T04:24:30Z" dateModified="2025-11-14T23:40:05Z" id="akaaṭ_04859ac8-fe4a-4b98-88d0-c7d9ad89ad15" guid="04859ac8-fe4a-4b98-88d0-c7d9ad89ad15">
