<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>اپَم </text></form>
</field>
<sense id="a931ccc8-4a8c-4d6d-b2b5-79dfae3b7157">
<grammatical-info value="Adjective">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>that who is not (there), absent</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>وہ جو موجود نہ ہو</text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>akho apám sese, mi bariṅ ayećán</text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>We will not speak of a people who are not here. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2019-08-26T04:18:32Z" dateModified="2025-11-21T15:52:17Z" id="apám2_67f1ba10-bd0c-4049-be91-edffd535fafe" guid="67f1ba10-bd0c-4049-be91-edffd535fafe">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>apám</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>It is the devoiced form of abam. The root bam means “was/were,” and the prefix a- marks negation, yielding abam (“was not/were not”). In actual speech, native speakers commonly devoice this to apam.</text></form>
</note>
<relation type="_component-lexeme" ref="b46a1995-2d40-4177-81e3-4aca740c30e0" order="0">
<trait name="is-primary" value="true"/>
<trait  name="complex-form-type" value="Antonym"/>
</relation>
<relation type="Compare" ref="apúm_5f3c3c93-403e-4ff9-be29-6f07f0e19b1d"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>apʌm</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>اپَم</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="851b8ab3-f264-4e70-8101-1e9facbc74e4" order="0">
<grammatical-info value="Verb">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>was not</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>(وہ) نہیں تھا</text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ne akho apám.</text></form>
<translation type="Back translation">
<form lang="en"><text>He was not here. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ja too apám</text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>I was not there. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
<sense id="e98a5ab2-9c58-4e17-b78d-8a0c16c9340b" order="1">
<grammatical-info value="Verb">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>were not, used only for humans (both SG. and PL)</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>نہیں تھے</text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>mi ses too apám. </text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>Our people were not there. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2021-04-13T04:12:34Z" dateModified="2025-11-21T15:57:18Z" id="apám ulle_3ed8c24f-520a-4d38-b586-2f7668e8fe66" guid="3ed8c24f-520a-4d38-b586-2f7668e8fe66">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>apám ulle</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>apám/abam ulle (in absence) vs bám ulle (in one's presence). In the case of 3SG.hf, apúm  is used. </text></form>
</note>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>apʌm uɫɛ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>اپَم اُڵے</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="950bfe56-a49f-4bac-a949-db1166e97eaf">
<grammatical-info value="Locative (ći, i)">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>in one's absentia </text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>غیر موجودگی میں</text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ne apám ulle ne ġa bo sen beé. </text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>Do not say anything to him in his absence. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ja akho apám ulle, un gute doro bota eta?</text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>Why did you do this work when I was not here?</text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
