<entry dateCreated="2019-10-24T13:53:42Z" dateModified="2025-11-22T14:51:51Z" id="ataleġ_db807ecd-feed-44a5-be62-e37dc1d42bf1" guid="db807ecd-feed-44a5-be62-e37dc1d42bf1">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ataleġ</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>atalɛɣ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>اتلیࣷغ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="79c157c2-c301-4d1d-a79e-f12361355dd8">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>A very important position in statecraft. Ataleġ used to be the head of military affairs. The word is erroneously mixed with the Persian word اتالیق (tutor or mentor).  Ataleġ is of an Uzbek or Turkic origin. Popular ataleġs have been Ataleġ Ghazi ( Yaqub Beg), a ruler and military adventurer from Central Asia, ataleġ Sarfraz Shah of Chitral, Ghufran Ataleġ of Yasin. Ataleġ must be well connected figure within different tribes as it was his job to prepare and unite the public for military adventures. </text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>اتالیق(ترکی)، سربراہ دفاع </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2019-12-25T02:19:50Z" dateModified="2025-11-22T21:21:16Z" id="ataśí_793570b2-deaf-4d1f-9680-6c409109efdf" guid="793570b2-deaf-4d1f-9680-6c409109efdf">
