<relation type="Compare" ref="heqai_0861dc2e-7b54-4f00-afc0-0aec0e8ef1ec"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="śiti_5a431b91-f8c5-40cd-a300-6b1ad63e18e6"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="shüťümah_ae205377-03b1-4ec0-8faf-62673edc5748"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>bɛnd</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>بیࣷند</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>iṅ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="149e52a1-fe3c-4cdd-bfaf-4c9630ca839c">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>In a trIn a traditional Buruśo house, the bend is the seating area located between the mán, héqai, and phéć. The bend is conventionally divided into three segments: 

- Right side (from the entrance): traditionally reserved for men. 
- Left side (from the entrance): traditionally reserved for women. 
- Central segment (from phéć to héqai): a shared space where both men and women sit together.

Seating arrangements within the bend are not governed by strict rules; rather, they follow customary practices passed down through generations. The bend is typically associated with wood flooring. When the same area lacks wood flooring, it is referred to as śiti</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>روائتی بروشو گھرمیں انگیٹھی  کے ارد گرد  بالائی حصے کو چھوڑ کر تین اطراف میں بیٹھنے کی جگہ کو بیࣷند کہتے ہیں۔</text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
