<entry dateCreated="2025-12-23T23:05:08Z" dateModified="2025-12-23T23:17:53Z" id="chám_df96535b-dbda-4361-bb32-71f165db27b7" guid="df96535b-dbda-4361-bb32-71f165db27b7">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>chám</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>chám can only be rendered naturally in English as an adjectival phrase meaning “a flow that has been stopped by an agent.” Its semantics are causative: the halted state results from someone’s intervention. This contrasts with its ergative counterpart 'duchám', which denotes “a flow that has stopped, on its own or possibly by someone. </text></form>
</note>
<relation type="Compare" ref="duchám_5d007f6e-49d6-4882-92d3-07f894a5622e"/>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Idioms"/>
<sense id="fe028007-9938-4db0-aa9b-16e32c6f5ad6">
<grammatical-info value="Adjective">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>(of a flow) what has been stopped, the stopped one</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>(بہاؤ) جسے روک دیاگیا ہے۔</text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>chám mulʰtam daa ayiduśimi.</text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>The stopped blood will not ooze again. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
