<entry dateCreated="2019-10-22T21:07:03Z" dateModified="2026-01-02T17:42:01Z" id="ćhardaġes_6bec91f3-de1d-4254-9b97-73442ee28658" guid="6bec91f3-de1d-4254-9b97-73442ee28658">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ćhardaġes</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>

Etymology (BUR): From ćhar “sound, noise, voice” + deexes “to cut through.” The compound likely refers to a wall that blocks, reduces, or cuts through sound—a traditional form of noise control. In modern terms, the concept resembles noise‑cancelling design, similar to how contemporary headphones reduce ambient noise.</text></form>
</note>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>tʃʰardaɣɛs</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>چھردا غیࣷس</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>-es,ha</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="2c3ead92-f3b0-494f-859d-b36e4062f86c">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>Two short interior walls that separate the heqai from the mán. These walls extend inward from the main exterior walls of the house and connect to two of the four main pillars at the lower end of the structure. The central area between the four pillars remains open, with no walls. Importantly, the walls of the ćhardaġes do not reach the ceiling—an intentional architectural feature that keeps the house airy and well‑lit.</text></gloss>
<trait name ="semantic-domain-ddp4" value="6.5.1 Building"/>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2020-02-04T23:12:44Z" dateModified="2026-01-02T17:44:54Z" id="ćharde_27fe0789-19da-4418-b887-d9cd307891ab" guid="27fe0789-19da-4418-b887-d9cd307891ab">
