<entry dateCreated="2023-12-18T04:24:59Z" dateModified="2025-12-30T02:06:17Z" id="ćop_d173cb67-e3ea-484f-b9fd-df4cd62bd63b" guid="d173cb67-e3ea-484f-b9fd-df4cd62bd63b">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ćop</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Idioms"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>tʃop</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>چوࣷپ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>ićiṅ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="55c124e1-c76d-42a2-8168-07e873449d0b">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>herbal skin protective paste. 

A traditional skin‑protective paste, similar in texture to a clay mask, made from burned goat horn or the flowers of the caper bush. Applied to the face and left to dry, it forms a natural barrier against ultraviolet radiation and extreme cold. Commonly used on the skin of newborn infants and postpartum mothers in high‑altitude communities.</text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>phuk dasene ćop an etum bu. </text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>The little girl has put on herbal paste on her face. </text></form>
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>چہرے پرلگانے کے لیے جڑی بوٹیوں کا حفاظتی لیپ</text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
