</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2020-04-26T20:46:02Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="daall-khaaṭ_2ced034a-6db1-47a8-8a9d-5b4ec5821191" guid="2ced034a-6db1-47a8-8a9d-5b4ec5821191">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>daall-khaaṭ</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>It literally means, up and down. The term has cultural connotation, linked with hospitality. In Burusho society, when a guest enters the house, the host will stand up to greet and will sit only after the guest is seated. Similarly, when the guest leaves, the host will usually  walk along to see off the guest. People are known to be on their toes to serve the guests. /daall-khāṭ/ therefore is used for hospitality. </text></form>
</note>
<relation type="Compare" ref="adáp_697bc7b8-de1b-466c-8fe3-4ad760ae3c4e"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>daːɫ-khaːʈ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>داڵ کھاٹ</text></form>
</field>
<trait name="Verb Types" value="INTR.Verbs"/>
<sense id="37957603-04c5-4758-927a-bfbed933f979">
<grammatical-info value="Verb">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>showing hospitality, serving</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>خاطر تواضع، مہان نوازی، خاطر داری</text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ses uṅya śuwa daall khaat guman. </text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>Serve the people well.</text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2020-04-12T17:26:35Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="daamaini_323bdb4f-18f2-4edd-826d-aa6120271aae" guid="323bdb4f-18f2-4edd-826d-aa6120271aae">
