<entry dateCreated="2024-05-01T03:29:11Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="dincheliki_016bf7f9-1cce-4f67-80e6-cf96572974a1" guid="016bf7f9-1cce-4f67-80e6-cf96572974a1">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>dincheliki</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Idioms"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>dintshɛliki</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>دِنڎھیلِکیࣷ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="965abd5e-8dbc-4677-939e-8e479768457b">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>After grinding makuk in a water mill, a small quantity of fresh wheat is immediately ground. This portion of flour is called dincheliki. The resulting flour is then combined with the makuk to make dishes. The reason for this is to neutralize any residual flavor likely to remain on the  surfaces of the millstone bed.  Dincheliki ensures that the mill is ready for other customers to grind fresh grain. </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
