<entry dateCreated="2020-05-15T20:05:52Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="gáṭ1_1c7607a5-45ff-4691-a781-2d2c6f282e88" guid="1c7607a5-45ff-4691-a781-2d2c6f282e88">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>gáṭ</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="sám_49475535-2a27-4187-84b1-62466d8e3d9b"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="taurić_131d8edd-fd2b-4163-9ce7-9681351d6098"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="chamáreṣ_43343061-840f-45aa-b929-ea4824c725f2"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>gʌʈ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>گٹ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="b594e649-aee6-44ff-9639-f5fd1c46136d">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>The central wood ceiling of the traditional Burusho house is designed with squares of wood-frames layered in quadrilateral altering positions where each upper frame is smaller than the lower.  Thus, the highest and the smallest frame is left open for smoke-hole or skylight.  Each beam of the last frame is called ‘chamareṣ’ and the opening smoke hole is called ‘sám’</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>چھت پر موجود روشن دان کا مربعی فریم جو لکڑی کا بنا ہوتا ہے۔ </text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>gáṭa sá wali.</text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>The sunlight fell on the ceiling (through the skylight)</text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
