<entry dateCreated="2020-02-04T06:58:32Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="ispandur_35a5fa98-28bf-4cb2-ab34-b83cf5272451" guid="35a5fa98-28bf-4cb2-ab34-b83cf5272451">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ispandur</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="doxná2_2190d2de-c6fc-4705-b62e-0c3874f3453f"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>ispandur</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>اِسپندُر</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>iṅ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="4436b16a-6c2c-4d0c-8c90-b83d37fa0067">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>wild rue, espand, Peganum harmala. Wild rue, also known as espand or Peganum harmala, holds significant cultural and spiritual value. Like the ancient Zoroastrians, the Burusho people burn espand on a bed of ashes and embers to purify individuals and their living spaces from evil spirits. This ritual bed, where espand seeds are sprinkled, is referred to as 'doxná.'  When the ispand̗ur is placed on the hot ashes, the seeds crackle, releasing a strong, aromatic smoke. During this process, the person performing the ritual chants 'uméh-uméh' while circling the d̗oxna around the individual to cleanse and rid them of evil influences."</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>انڈین ہارمالہ، اسپند</text></gloss>
<trait name ="semantic-domain-ddp4" value="1.5 Plant"/>
<field type="scientific-name">
<form lang="en"><text>Peganum harmala</text></form>
</field>
<illustration href="ispandur-harmel-espand-Peganum harmala.JPG">
<label>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ispand̗ur</text></form>
</label>
</illustration></sense>
</entry>
