<entry dateCreated="2022-09-21T14:00:23Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="ísur_b6e15a74-86bb-4a40-86cf-1f4cbdcdc4de" guid="b6e15a74-86bb-4a40-86cf-1f4cbdcdc4de">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ísur</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="hur2_e9d31302-5cf0-4200-b302-df51f6b8e370"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="iss_789c5ba9-3c89-4d74-b6c4-82f2140b7326"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="iṣkor_ebbe4c5a-9463-4cf0-80cd-f9de74c4081f"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>ɪsur</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>اِسُر</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="3cbb12ee-68c5-486d-88e7-a4a3c759db06">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>Contraction  of /is-hur/, an offshoot furrow, parallel to the main irrigation furrow of the field, used to apply controlled amount of water simultaneously to multiple nodes of ground patches.  This technique is used to optimize the water resource, and to avert flooding in the field.  Literally the word  /ísur/ is derived from /ís/ and /húr/ which means: child of the furrow. </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
