<entry dateCreated="2019-12-30T20:53:13Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="mati2_119add88-2996-4455-8e1b-137911060d87" guid="119add88-2996-4455-8e1b-137911060d87">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>mati</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>mati</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>متی</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="51884c24-e8c3-47e0-928e-f139022e7b11">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>An apricot's kernel (bitter) is burned and abraded against a stone to produce a paste or  mash. This black paste is called /mati/. The paste is applied on wounded or chapped skin. Primitive wisdom of burning the kernel is to free the paste of an unknown chemical component.</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>خوبانی  کے گیری کو جلا کر کریم بنائی جائی ہے  اور زخم  یا پھٹی کٹی جلد پر لگائی جاتی ہے جسے متی کہا جاتا ہے۔  </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
