<entry dateCreated="2020-03-11T05:25:34Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="máyacharʰćum_55e5dc9c-2317-4d0b-b97c-513e8ca290ee" guid="55e5dc9c-2317-4d0b-b97c-513e8ca290ee">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>máyacharʰćum</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="oyaṅuśćum_b71c8e85-4d7d-4149-b927-473318cbf337"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>mʌyatshar̥tʃum</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>موࣷیَڎھرْچُم</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="03e2ae59-4bb0-4173-ac75-c8ad4621c93a">
<grammatical-info value="Verb/Verbal Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>A bride’s farewell visit to her close neighbors is a tradition called móyachrʰćum.  Accompanied by her friends, she visits each home in the neighborhood, where she is warmly received and presented with gifts. Historically, these tokens of goodwill included small household essentials such as cups, plates, and other practical items to help her settle into her new home.</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>دلھن کا رخصتی سے پیلے ہمسائیوں سے ملنے جانا جہاں ہمسائیوں کی طرف سے اُسے تحفے میں کچھ نہ کچھ دیا جاتا ہے۔ یہ ایک رواج ہے جسے مُیاࣷڎھرْچُم کہتے ہیں۔  </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2019-04-19T02:17:44Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="mayaġa_ac026011-8d2f-4451-8d99-64014cd387f3" guid="ac026011-8d2f-4451-8d99-64014cd387f3">
