<entry dateCreated="2024-04-13T14:52:47Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="Muḍúri_3909254e-af8c-49a1-b08b-e77cd729112d" guid="3909254e-af8c-49a1-b08b-e77cd729112d">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>Muḍúri</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>In Yásin, near the upper Sandi village, lies a strategically significant hill that once served as a fortified stronghold. According to local accounts, this fortification was established by Shah Tahir, an exiled ruler from Badakhshan. During times of war, the hill became a crucial military stronghold for the local people. The fort remained intact throughout the reign of Gohar Aman, the revered ruler of Yásin. However, during the rule of Raja Mulk Aman, Kashmiri troops, along with allied forces from Gilgit-Baltistan, launched a devastating assault on the area. They committed widespread atrocities and ultimately destroyed the fort. In the absence of his uncle Mulk Aman, local commander Duri Aman assumed charge but was killed along with approximately 1,500 locals, many of whom were women and children. The brutal actions of the Kashmiri forces were later documented and reported to the international media by British explorer G.W. Hayward. Facing exposure and embarrassment, the Maharaja of Kashmir orchestrated Hayward’s assassination in Darʰkut, Yásin.</text></form>
</note>
<relation type="Compare" ref="grám_396822b9-aa02-4586-82ee-209002553414"/>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Idioms"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>muɖʊri</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>مُڈُری</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="87612514-831d-430a-acc4-8146c5a600ab">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>a  historic place in Yasin</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>یسیࣷن میں ایک جگے کانام</text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
