<entry dateCreated="2020-02-06T15:41:09Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="paitaxti_ec44e24f-fa78-4ec8-9fee-6ee1dccfbace" guid="ec44e24f-fa78-4ec8-9fee-6ee1dccfbace">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>paitaxti</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>paitaxti</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>پیتختیࣷ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>miṅ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="8a82208e-91c0-4684-8a35-0f58dbb38a6c">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>Paitaxt, in Persian, refers to the federal capital. In religious contexts, however, it carries a deeper significance—symbolizing the central authority. The term Paitaxti represents a token of allegiance to that authority. Within Burusho societies, this central figure is most often the Imam of the time. Traditionally, Paitaxti is bestowed upon the Imam’s representatives, known as pirs, particularly upon their return from an audience with the Imam at his residence. </text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>انعام و اکرام یا تحفہ جو پیر کو پیش کیا جاتا ہے جب وہ امام سے مل کر واپس آجائے یا کسی دورے کے بعد واپس آجائے۔ </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2020-05-24T16:32:26Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="pakaar_ac598bc8-3068-4db0-a8ac-9e307cd8ad8d" guid="ac598bc8-3068-4db0-a8ac-9e307cd8ad8d">
