<entry dateCreated="2024-07-16T16:47:23Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="parʰpi_01a1c21b-e1a6-4f3a-b238-ff34382e4524" guid="01a1c21b-e1a6-4f3a-b238-ff34382e4524">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>parʰpi</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Idioms"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>par̥pi</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>برْپیࣷ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>miṅ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="2d9faeb3-7c0c-400b-8cdc-c4aac97f65e7" order="0">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>The parʰpi refers to the deepest root of the caper bush, a rare and prized find due to the plant’s remarkable ability to develop an extensive and deeply embedded root system. It is typically uncovered only when a large mound of earth collapses, revealing the hidden root beneath the surface. </text></gloss>
</sense>
<sense id="dec162c0-8add-48ef-be3b-068d8ea4fc87" order="1">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>rare</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>نایاب</text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
