<entry dateCreated="2020-01-02T00:19:59Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="payen_d543c4fb-ecfd-4dd6-b49c-67b107686cb0" guid="d543c4fb-ecfd-4dd6-b49c-67b107686cb0">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>payen</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>payɛn</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>پَئیࣷن</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="01219d54-6efb-4a5b-b7ec-1392fe508d9e">
<grammatical-info value="Adjective">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>In the primitive barter system of the Burusho people, the unit of price was known as payén. One payén was equivalent to a sheep, two payén to a goat, and twelve payén to an ox. The term is likely derived from the Khowar word pai, meaning goat, reflecting the central role of livestock in their economic exchanges.</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>قیمت کا پیمانہ۔ ایک پین ایک بھیڑ کے برابر ہوتا ہے۔ دو پین بکرے کے اور بارہ پین بیل کےبرابر </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
