<entry dateCreated="2019-12-27T04:34:59Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="phute tariṅ_21478d0f-3e68-4645-b2a1-b7369aa4483c" guid="21478d0f-3e68-4645-b2a1-b7369aa4483c">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>phute tariṅ</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="tárʰc_6c2ca61a-008d-49d6-aa38-a6a9e7ec5a7c"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>pʰutɛ tariŋ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>پُھوتے ترِݩ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>iṅ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="0623f1e6-bd3f-4cc7-8d7f-34c1ace383c4">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>bladder-senna, The literal meaning of phute tariṅ in Burushaski is the bladder/churn of /phut/, a mythological being.  Due to  the inflated, puffy pod of the shrub, phute tariṅ became common among the Burusho people. </text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>ایک قسم کی   ایشائی   جھاڑی جس پر  مشکے جیسی ذرد    پھلیاں لگتی ہیں  </text></gloss>
<trait name ="semantic-domain-ddp4" value="1.5 Plant"/>
<field type="scientific-name">
<form lang="en"><text>Colutea arborescens (Baguenaudier)</text></form>
</field>
<illustration href="phut taring.JPG">
<label>
<form lang="bsk"><text>phute tariṅ</text></form>
</label>
</illustration></sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2020-04-30T16:47:50Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="phuto ohuyaćum_f72bdbb2-f512-4e34-858e-b5437ff27511" guid="f72bdbb2-f512-4e34-858e-b5437ff27511">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>phuto ohuyaćum</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>pʰuto ohuyatʃum</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>پُھوتو اوࣷہُیاࣷچُم</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="ac04d534-9b5c-4d00-952e-8e8689606ed2">
<grammatical-info value="Verb/Verbal Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>evicting photo (plural of phút). A pre-isl tradition where the mythological being called phut is evicted from one's home through a ritual called Photo Ohuyaćum (making the phúts run away). This tradition is called Jashtan in khowar, more or less similar to Jashtakhan in Kalash culture</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>ایک رسم کا نام، پھُت یا پھتوں کو بگانا یا بے دخل کرنا۔</text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2020-04-19T19:18:35Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="phuṭ_5608b598-0b17-4338-9030-82e0b1086c04" guid="5608b598-0b17-4338-9030-82e0b1086c04">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>phuṭ</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>pʰuʈ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>پُھٹ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>iṅ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="b1bf1c6c-5835-4e4c-93f6-c85b8c2aba62">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>glance, brief and hurried look, cursory look</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>سرسری نگاہ، ایک نظر </text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>un hek ja ayakalla phuṭ eta. </text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>Would you please take a glance at me. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
