<entry dateCreated="2021-02-21T01:09:52Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="piriṭis1_f400d35b-2071-4cd4-94ff-a6e4cfafe680" guid="f400d35b-2071-4cd4-94ff-a6e4cfafe680">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>piriṭis</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>piriʈis</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>پِریٹِس</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>iśu</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="da4ef0d7-24f4-45af-9b5d-3550089729f1">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>spirit of hearth or fire. When the embers in the hearth are mysteriously blown at night (probably by air), it is said the piritis is blowing the embers.  The concept is, more or less, similar to the Tibetan hearth God, Thab-Ilha which interestingly correlates to two Burushaski words, thap (night) and ballhas (vampire). Before going to bed, the hearth ashes and embers are organized so as to carry the embers to the next morning. Unsuccessful carrying of the embers to the next morning was considered a bad omen, and hence petition was made to the Pritis for protection of the fire. In Yásin,before leaving the house, especially on journey, a symbolic ritual is performed where a person would touch the hearth stone and then would touch his/her chin and forehead. Hearth and fire rituals are integral part of the Burusho society. </text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>آگ کی دیوی، آگ کا رکھوالا </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2020-03-08T00:44:56Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="piriṭis2_34b21284-cee5-4db0-8df8-9dbc71a69635" guid="34b21284-cee5-4db0-8df8-9dbc71a69635">
