<entry dateCreated="2022-02-02T16:46:09Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="poc̣aali_b3e834c9-17ab-4cdf-ad0f-bedbb5560fd6" guid="b3e834c9-17ab-4cdf-ad0f-bedbb5560fd6">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>poc̣aali</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>puc̣ in Shina means son [Indic: put, puter, putra]  and āli means 'came'. It could be a Shina loan word. </text></form>
</note>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>poʈʂaːli</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>پوڅالی</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>miṅ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="8c312244-2bfb-48b7-8231-783c3412c3c3">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>On the arrival of a newborn, the mother is visited by her parents or maternal relatives who bring a valuable and beneficial gift to her. This tradition is called Poc̣ali. </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
