<entry dateCreated="2020-05-08T05:51:05Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="raṅjuli_fe10bf14-095e-4096-a4f4-48cfde8fcf33" guid="fe10bf14-095e-4096-a4f4-48cfde8fcf33">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>raṅjuli</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>raŋʒuli</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>رنجُلیࣷ</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>mo</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="c6b6ff2c-4a49-4348-8304-d94734b4db69">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>a type of flower, popular in Burusho tribes. The flower is considered a symbol of love and beauty. There is a folk art in Indian culture called Rangoli. It primarily depicts an unknown flower and rangoli is displayed in Hindu festivals. The pattern is mythologically sacred.  Rangoli according to Indian etymologists has originated from Sanskrit word rangavalli which in Burushaski means 'fell in color'. Arabic dictionaries have used ranjuli (راجنولی) for the Indian art Rangoli. It is very likely, Rangjuli was once religiously revered in Burusho societies as rangavali, and Sanskrit might have borrowed it. </text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>ایک پھول کا نام   </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
