<entry dateCreated="2023-12-15T15:01:25Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="sellmen2_40d7237d-ad29-46d2-a68f-ed67ec264c23" guid="40d7237d-ad29-46d2-a68f-ed67ec264c23">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>sellmen</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="gálldir_29f2aae1-6e76-43c9-9fcc-e886bb10a41a"/>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Idioms"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>sɛɫmɛn</text></form>
</field>
<trait name="Noun Class" value="duwa"/>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>سیࣷڵمیࣷن</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>iṅ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="c5b6bdbc-fe3c-4664-b082-118725a002f0">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>A ceremonial juniper branch used in springtime rituals among Burushaski-speaking communities. Traditionally cut fresh from highland areas, the sellmen is kept outdoors and only brought indoors after the preparation of a sacred dish called makuti (made from sprouted wheat flour). It is decorated with indigenous agricultural tools—such as anjan, gilʰtir, plow shear replicas—and natural elements like willow catkins or blossoms. The tree holds religious sanctity and symbolizes renewal and reverence for ancestral traditions.</text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2019-03-05T17:18:52Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="sén2_e9da8796-8df1-4491-9d67-206abe8a0a7d" guid="e9da8796-8df1-4491-9d67-206abe8a0a7d">
