<entry dateCreated="2022-10-31T17:38:55Z" dateModified="2025-11-12T03:53:03Z" id="-sk-_a5f491e4-aff4-4ffd-9b25-c8838c1c8a09" guid="a5f491e4-aff4-4ffd-9b25-c8838c1c8a09">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>-sk-</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="infix"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="-darú_dbc02590-53f0-4787-8feb-0ec8c218a3b3"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="aaskus_724627af-aec1-4103-a0b6-759471aa4eb7"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="aaskir_395ed2b7-eeb5-4851-bc00-fcedb6751a6c"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>-sk-</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>سک</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="d481fdea-0aa3-47a0-9d65-2fe92450a16a">
<gloss lang="en"><text>Used with long-vowel pronominal prefixes to indicate possession, this clitic forms kinship terms meaning “in-law.”

- eeskir (ee–sk–hir, aa–sk–hir, moo–sk–hir, oo–sk–hir) → father-in-law of the possessor 
- eeskus (ee–sk–gus, aa–sk–gus, moo–sk–gus, oo–sk–gus) → mother-in-law of the possessor

The plural form for both male and female in-laws is –sk–indaru, which also requires pronominal possession. During elision, the consonants /h/ and /g/ following /k/ may weaken or drop. Instead of the words aa-sk-hir and aa-sk-gus, the prevalent terms are aaskir and askus. </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2021-02-20T05:47:15Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="-skaraac̣um_a84b2e21-2031-435b-a72e-ca04b832edd1" guid="a84b2e21-2031-435b-a72e-ca04b832edd1">
