<entry dateCreated="2019-04-01T02:29:22Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="shüťümah_ae205377-03b1-4ec0-8faf-62673edc5748" guid="ae205377-03b1-4ec0-8faf-62673edc5748">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>śutum</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="bend2_7c62d3d3-8ec7-457d-af3c-23e22c71c650"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>ʃutum</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>شُتُم</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Plural Suffix">
<form lang="bsk"><text>aa</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="27ceeedb-4ea5-4b25-85d2-20f792bb5491">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>One of the traditional hearthstones, known as śutuma, is part of a three-stone arrangement placed in a U-shape around the fire. These stones serve both to support cooking pots and to help organize the wood fire efficiently. Among the Burusho people of Yásin, the use of śutuma remains common for both indoor and outdoor cooking—even as of 2019. However, modern cooking appliances, such as stoves, have largely replaced the śutuma in many households.</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>وہ  پتھر  جن  پر کھانا پکایا جاتا ہے۔</text></gloss>
<trait name ="semantic-domain-ddp4" value="6.5.3 Building materials"/>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2020-02-02T07:03:15Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="śuṭi_2166287b-6232-46ff-affe-a0b7ac765e1c" guid="2166287b-6232-46ff-affe-a0b7ac765e1c">
