</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>تلٚغوࣷ</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="df94686f-ee5a-4c59-ad08-150a308b34a0">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>loosely twisted yarn (of yak or goat's hair). This loosely twisted yarn is later spun with spindle to make a proper yarn. </text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2020-07-22T21:20:00Z" dateModified="2025-11-04T15:04:16Z" id="tallġo diwusaas_e1461d23-1052-480c-a086-7557c7ef5ef2" guid="e1461d23-1052-480c-a086-7557c7ef5ef2">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>tallġo diwusaas</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="phrase"/>
<relation type="_component-lexeme" ref="">
<trait  name="complex-form-type" value="Unspecified Complex Form"/>
</relation>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>taɫɣo diwusaːs</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>تلٚغو دِوُساس</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="bd38f8ea-3ffc-414e-a17c-ddba707c39df">
<grammatical-info value="Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>It is traditional V-shaped spinning staff used in a two-person process to spin coarse goat hair. One person walks backward while twisting the fiber, using the tallġo to guide and tension the strand, while a second person feeds unspun hair into the fork. Once a length is spun, the yarn is coiled onto a distaff. This method, rooted in pastoral lifeways, allows for long, continuous threads without mechanical tools—ideal for producing durable yarn for rugs, ropes, and outerwear.</text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>تر کُٹی</text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
