<entry dateCreated="2019-10-26T18:13:32Z" dateModified="2025-11-07T20:21:56Z" id="thám desukećum _77222e26-4e9d-4f21-96c5-08cdf7d37e41" guid="77222e26-4e9d-4f21-96c5-08cdf7d37e41">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>thám desukećum</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="phrase"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>thʌm dɛsukɛtʃum </text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>تھم دیࣷسُک ایࣷچُم</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="9ec53d29-f688-491c-81c8-c7927695737e">
<grammatical-info value="Verb/Verbal Noun">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>Hosting a thám (local ruler) at one’s home was a gesture of respect and hospitality. During travels, a thám would often rest or spend the night at someone’s house. The Burushaski verb desúkećum means “to help someone dismount,” typically from a horse. Thus, thám desúkećum likely refers to the act of assisting the ruler in dismounting—symbolically and practically—so he may rest or lodge for the night</text></gloss>
<definition>
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>بادشاہ یا حکمران کی میزبانی کے لیے اپنے گھرا قیام کروانا۔</text></form>
</definition>
</sense>
</entry>
<entry dateCreated="2022-11-13T02:30:23Z" dateModified="2025-11-07T21:09:15Z" id="thamam_424229a8-4d2c-4ea1-9bbb-09bda23cd6c3" guid="424229a8-4d2c-4ea1-9bbb-09bda23cd6c3">
