<entry dateCreated="2024-04-30T05:35:42Z" dateModified="2025-10-30T14:35:20Z" id="umeh!_076df7d9-066b-4ae3-b86f-70c776bd2a27" guid="076df7d9-066b-4ae3-b86f-70c776bd2a27">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>umeh!</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="stem"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>Uméh Uméh” is a fascinating and likely ancient term in Burushaski, believed to be a ritual invocation—perhaps directed toward an unknown deity or mythical force. In antiquity, the Burusho people of Yásin were said to chant Uméh Uméh upon encountering a mongoose, especially when the animal appeared near milk, a substance symbolizes purity and protection. In another striking ritual, when a mudflood surged toward a home, Burusho women would take a cup of milk, dip their fingers into it, and walk toward the oncoming flood. They would sprinkle the milk in its path while chanting Uméh Uméh, as if appealing to unseen powers to divert or calm the destructive force. Umeh is also chanted with the burning of esphand, which is used to wad off bad spirits (see under ispandur). According to Quwat K. Sunny, the vedic word Om, a religious chanting still prevalent in jainism and hinduism has its roots in the Burushaski word Uméh or Oméh. </text></form>
</note>
<trait name="do-not-publish-in" value="Idioms"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>umɛh!</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>اُمیࣷح</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="7f909c1f-2649-431a-8456-6da92a3390a8">
<grammatical-info value="Exclamation">
</grammatical-info>
<gloss lang="en"><text>so be it!, an utterance after or before a prayer, amen </text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>دعائیہ کلمہ، آمین!</text></gloss>
</sense>
</entry>
