<entry dateCreated="2022-10-31T18:26:44Z" dateModified="2025-11-11T22:16:58Z" id="-xol-_95808e01-bdd7-4bb2-adb6-278a7c81d912" guid="95808e01-bdd7-4bb2-adb6-278a7c81d912">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>-xol-</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="infix"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>The root –xol– expresses pain or hurting, but never in isolation. It is semantically bound to the experiencer's body part —there is no concept of pain as a detached event or property. Instead, pain is always possessed, felt, or located in some part.

• No impersonal usage: You cannot say “there is pain” without specifying who feels it and where
• Pronominal prefix required: The verb must be prefixed with person/number markers (e.g., a–xoli–ni = “something of mine hurt". The verb is always used following the name of the body part, also Possessed (e.g a-ren ='my hand')</text></form>
</note>
<relation type="Compare" ref="ǩholüm_4de8aa53-07b3-4839-a433-9e40e5609942"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>-xol-</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>خوࣷل</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="44774634-2012-4bd7-a45d-59a922a048bb">
<gloss lang="en"><text>root word for pain or hurting, always used with pronominal prefixes. There is no concept of pain a part of body without the concept of its possessor. The stem -xol- thus is used inalienably. </text></gloss>
<gloss lang="ur-PK"><text>درد ہونے کے لیےاساسی لفظ</text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ja aren axolum duwa. </text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>My hand is hurting. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>goo gós goxolimi. (go+xol-imi)</text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>Your heart or stomach will hurt. </text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
