<entry dateCreated="2021-01-21T17:26:43Z" dateModified="2025-10-08T01:12:46Z" id="-yu_4186b72e-0cd3-4068-86fb-ef68997a8192" guid="4186b72e-0cd3-4068-86fb-ef68997a8192">
<lexical-unit>
<form lang="bsk"><text>-yu</text></form>
</lexical-unit>
<trait  name="morph-type" value="suffix"/>
<note>
<form lang="en"><text>Nouns and adjectives ending with 'en' may be exceptions to the aforementioned rule. For the purpose of pluralization, the 'en' is replaced with 'iu'. Examples are: c̣hen, phen, khen (c̣hiu, phiu, khiu). This exception has something to do with aspirated stressed syllable that the word starts with. As we observe that the word ten is not pluralized as 'tiu'. Ten is pluralized as tenjo. </text></form>
</note>
<relation type="Compare" ref="-śku_de866746-5d28-4478-9399-77a01e3ff248"/>
<relation type="Compare" ref="-mo_7c4105ab-22eb-4262-bfb7-2d477fad65a9"/>
<field type="IPA Burushaski">
<form lang="bsk"><text>-yu</text></form>
</field>
<field type="Perso Arabic Burushaski">
<form lang="ur-PK"><text>یُو</text></form>
</field>
<sense id="f9cc6c62-b940-4363-996e-a3d67069ccb6">
<gloss lang="en"><text>It is one of the Pluralization suffixes in Burushaski. This suffix is applied to nouns in the bai, bu (h) and bi (x) classes that end with the consonant 'n'. For the pluralization of such nouns, the final 'n' of the Singular noun is dropped and replaced it with '-yu'. The criteria is the same for Adjectives referring to the h and x classes of the nouns. Example: con śillan &gt; coyu śillayu. </text></gloss>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>asumun, asumuyu</text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>star, stars</text></form>
</translation>
</example>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ġawumun, ġawumuyu</text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>planet, planets</text></form>
</translation>
</example>
<example>
<form lang="bsk"><text>ġwun, ġawuyu</text></form>
<translation type="Free translation">
<form lang="en"><text>muskmelon, muskmelons</text></form>
</translation>
</example>
</sense>
</entry>
